  SNDK [neutral]: Sandisk completed its 8-for-1 stock split, and headlines from November 26 describe its shares as jumping after Nvidia tested Kioxia flash drives in an AI rack. Other reports cite a Goldman Sachs target cut tied to lower 2028 memory prices, and ETFs bought $18.1 million of SanDisk stock on November 24.
  MU [neutral]: Micron (MU) shares have fallen sharply since June on fears of a memory cycle peak, and fell 4.53% after TrendForce cut its 2027 memory forecasts. Headlines also cite a possible new challenge from Huawei in homegrown high-bandwidth memory, while some analysts and commentators call the stock cheap and say memory shortages may last into 2028.
  DELL [neutral]: Dell reported a record $54.9 billion quarter with AI server revenue up 291% to $22 billion and a $132.3 billion backlog, but BofA cut its price target by $100 and questioned AI growth sustainability. Separately, Nvidia told partners to pause server shipments to Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand and Indonesia, and GuruFocus reported that Dell and Super Micro buyers in Southeast Asia were told their Nvidia server orders cannot ship. Dell's stock has reportedly fallen more than a third in a month.
  HPE [neutral]: HPE reported a record quarter and raised its fiscal 2027 revenue growth outlook to a midpoint of 31%, and it also announced a dividend hike. Despite the beat-and-raise results, the stock plunged after a comment from its finance chief, and investors remain concerned about an AI spending slowdown.
  SPCX [neutral]: The FCC conditionally approved SpaceX's Starmind constellation of up to 1 million satellites for orbital data centers, with a seven-year deadline for the first satellite and full deployment by 2040. Separately, SpaceX and Elon Musk said they will give one share each to U.S. children and active Trump Accounts, and Cathie Wood's ARK bought more shares. A Motley Fool piece argued that the current price leaves little room for error.
  PLTR [neutral]: Palantir shares have fallen more than 20% from their October high in a month of AI and tech sell-offs, even though third-quarter revenue rose 96% and the company reported its best quarter ever. Director Peter Thiel disclosed a $122 million share purchase made under a trading plan, and the stock has risen about 14% since that purchase. Michael Burry said he kept put options on Palantir while exiting his Nasdaq-100 short.

